//? Title: Kolmogorov–Smirnov tests and empirical CDFs
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Simulate two sensor populations centered at zero, with different spreads.
The two-sample KS test compares their full distributions. One-sample calls
compare the first sample to a prespecified reference Normal distribution
through both the convenience API and the general CDF API.
)

> Simulate, test and plot

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/distribution_comparisons.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sp = dm::stats;
using dm::graphics::RPlot;

int main() {
    dm::set_seed(51);
    const auto x = dm::rnorm(0, 1, 160);
    const auto y = dm::rnorm(0, 2.4, 160);
    const auto two_sample = sp::ks_test_two_sample(x, y);
    const auto reference = sp::ks_test_one_sample_normal(x);
    const auto supplied_cdf = sp::ks_test_one_sample(x, [](double value) {
        return dm::random::normal_cdf(value);
    });
    std::print("| Comparison | D | p-value |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n"
               "| Two independent samples | {:.3f} | {:.4g} |\n"
               "| First sample vs N(0, 1) | {:.3f} | {:.4g} |\n"
               "| Same null via CDF callback | {:.3f} | {:.4g} |\n",
               two_sample.statistic, two_sample.p_value,
               reference.statistic, reference.p_value, supplied_cdf.statistic, supplied_cdf.p_value);

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    sp::empirical_cdf_plot(x, y, "SD 1", "SD 2.4").save_svg("build/distribution_comparisons.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Comparison | D | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Two independent samples | 0.244 | 0.0001121 |
| First sample vs N(0, 1) | 0.033 | 0.994 |
| Same null via CDF callback | 0.033 | 0.994 |
\image(figures/distribution_comparisons.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Empirical CDFs of the simulated populations show divergence in both tails.)
\alttext(Empirical CDFs of the simulated populations show divergence in both tails.)

> Interpretation

KS measures the largest vertical separation between CDFs. The samples are
generated with the same population location, so a distributional difference
can reflect spread alone. One-sample KS calibration requires fixing the
reference distribution independently of the data. The p-values here use
asymptotic references.

> References

- [R Kolmogorov–Smirnov tests|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html]
